{
  // Secondary agents — very specific / niche single-purpose specialists.
  "subAgents": {
    "data_analyst": {
      "description": "Computes over already-collected structured data files — CSV, JSON, XLSX spreadsheets, exported metrics snapshots — to produce aggregations, group-bys, trends, KPIs, and statistical anomalies, preserving exact values, units, column names, and caveats. Reads files in the workspace and runs numeric tool pipelines on them; it does NOT query live databases (route SQL/Postgres/MySQL work to sql_specialist), does NOT render the numbers as charts or tables (chart_designer does that), and does NOT reconcile conflicting prose sources (evidence_analyst does that). Hands cleaned figures to chart_designer for plotting. Example queries: 'compute monthly revenue from this CSV', 'group this dataset by region and average the spend', 'analysiere die Verkaufszahlen aus der Excel-Tabelle', 'welche KPIs weichen vom Trend ab', 'flag anomalies in this metrics export', 'read the spreadsheet and compute averages by category'.",
      "capabilities": ["structured-data analysis", "csv computation", "json data inspection", "spreadsheet aggregation", "group-by and pivot", "trend analysis", "statistical anomaly detection", "kpi computation"],
      "tags": ["data", "analysis", "csv", "json", "xlsx", "spreadsheet", "metrics", "kpi", "trend", "aggregation", "statistics", "tabular"],
      "model": {
        "enableThinking": true,
        "temperature": 0.1,
        "maxTokens": 6144
      },
      "maxIterations": 10,
      "container": { "disabled": true }, // Tier 0/1 tools only — in-process is safe
      "systemPrompt": "You analyze structured data and tabular evidence. Prefer extracted file content, raw file reads, and workspace search before writing conclusions. Preserve exact numeric values, units, date ranges, column names, and caveats. Distinguish clearly between observed data, derived interpretation, and missing data. When a calculation or summary matters to other agents, publish it via share_finding with enough context to be reused without reopening the source files.",
      "tools": [
        "extract_file_content",
        "read_file",
        "list_files",
        "workspace_search",
        "read_shared_facts",
        "share_finding",
        "write_file",
        "research_note",
        "research_notes_read",
        "research_notes_summary",
        "research_notes_clear",
        "metric_write",
        "metric_query",
        "graph_query",
        "spreadsheet_read",
        "json_query",
        "regex_test",
        "datetime_arithmetic",
        "hash_compute",
        "run_tool_pipeline"
      ]
    },
    "log_analyst": {
      "description": "Reads log TEXT and extracts patterns from it: structured JSON logs, journald output, nginx/web access logs, application stack traces, and container logs (via read_file, journalctl/docker-logs over ssh, or kubectl_logs). Surfaces error clusters, request-rate and latency outliers, authentication-failure bursts, and reconstructs an incident timeline by tracing correlation IDs across services. Its job is reading and correlating the log lines themselves — distinct from ops_triage, which diagnoses a live failing service end-to-end (health checks, config drift, provider/gateway faults) and may call on this agent for the log-reading slice. Example queries: 'why did error rate spike yesterday at 14:00', 'analysiere die nginx-Logs auf ungewöhnliche Zugriffe', 'trace the root cause through these container logs', 'identify the slowest requests in the last hour from the access log', 'correlate this error ID across the service logs'.",
      "capabilities": ["log parsing", "error pattern extraction", "log anomaly detection", "latency outlier analysis", "access log analysis", "stack-trace triage", "security-log signal detection", "incident timeline reconstruction", "correlation id tracing"],
      "tags": ["logs", "log-analysis", "errors", "stacktrace", "journald", "nginx", "container-logs", "access-log", "latency", "correlation-id", "incident"],
      "model": {
        "enableThinking": true,
        "temperature": 0.1,
        "maxTokens": 8192
      },
      "maxIterations": 14,
      "container": { "disabled": true }, // read-only tools only — in-process is safe
      "systemPrompt": "You analyse log files and streaming log output to surface actionable insights. Read log content via read_file for workspace files, or ssh_exec with journalctl/docker-logs commands for live system logs. For Kubernetes-backed services, prefer kubectl_logs with --tail and --since bounds over copying logs off the cluster — use --previous to inspect the prior container instance when the pod has crashed — always against the configured external cluster. For structured JSON logs, parse line by line and group by level, service, and error code. For access logs, extract: request rate per path, error rate (4xx/5xx), top slow requests, unusual user agents, and any burst patterns. For application logs, identify: exception traces, repeated failures, timeout chains, and correlation IDs that link events across services. For security logs, flag: authentication failures, unusual IP bursts, privilege escalation events, and unexpected service restarts. Work through the log in time order; identify the earliest error signal and trace forward to find what it triggered. Summarize findings as a structured incident timeline when an anomaly chain exists. Publish evidence with exact timestamps, line references, and severity classifications via share_finding so ops_triage can act without re-reading the raw log.",
      "tools": [
        "read_file",
        "list_files",
        "workspace_search",
        "ssh_exec",
        "shell_exec",
        "kubectl_logs",
        "kubectl_get",
        "prometheus_query",
        "read_shared_facts",
        "share_finding",
        "metric_write",
        "metric_query",
        "write_file"
      ]
    },
    "document_intake": {
      "description": "Extracts exact text, tables, and metadata out of BINARY or non-plaintext attachments and prepares them as reusable facts for the rest of the swarm: PDFs (incl. scanned/OCR and fillable forms), DOCX, XLSX/CSV spreadsheets, images, audio and video (transcription), Jupyter notebooks (.ipynb), email files (.eml/.mbox), and iCalendar (.ics). It is the FIRST step that turns an uploaded file into machine-readable content; it does not write summaries for the user (summarizer does that on already-readable text) and does not reconcile conflicting sources (evidence_analyst does that). Example queries: 'extract all line items from this invoice PDF', 'fill in this PDF form', 'was steht auf der gescannten Rechnung', 'Tabelle aus dem Excel-Sheet extrahieren', 'walk through this Jupyter notebook', 'lies das .eml und gib mir den Inhalt', 'parse the .ics into structured events', 'transcribe the meeting recording video'.",
      "capabilities": ["document intake", "attachment extraction", "file-format classification", "metadata extraction", "exact text extraction", "PDF form filling", "spreadsheet reading", "OCR preparation", "jupyter notebook extraction", "email-file parsing", "icalendar parsing", "audio and video transcription"],
      "tags": ["documents", "files", "intake", "attachments", "pdf", "docx", "xlsx", "csv", "ipynb", "notebook", "eml", "mbox", "email-file", "ics", "icalendar", "audio", "video", "transcription", "extraction", "ocr", "metadata"],
      "model": {
        "enableThinking": true,
        "temperature": 0.1,
        "maxTokens": 6144
      },
      "maxIterations": 10,
      "container": { "disabled": true }, // Tier 0/1 tools only — in-process is safe
      "systemPrompt": "You prepare documents for the rest of the swarm. Start by identifying what files are present, their formats, likely purpose, date range, authorship signals, and whether they look narrative, tabular, scanned, or mixed. Pick the right extractor by file extension: extract_file_content for PDFs and office files, extract_notebook for .ipynb (it preserves code + outputs as a single Markdown doc), extract_email for .eml or .mbox (decodes quoted-printable, lists attachments), extract_calendar for .ics, transcribe_audio for audio files, transcribe_video for .mp4/.mov/.webm/.mkv, spreadsheet_read for .xlsx/.csv, and analyze_image when screenshots, scans, or embedded figures matter. Normalize the result into reusable findings: what the document is, what period it covers, what sections or tables matter, what data is missing, and which downstream specialist should use it next. Publish enough structured intake facts via share_finding that other agents can work from the intake summary instead of reopening every attachment.",
      "tools": [
        "extract_file_content",
        "extract_notebook",
        "extract_email",
        "extract_calendar",
        "transcribe_audio",
        "transcribe_video",
        "analyze_image",
        "read_file",
        "list_files",
        "list_pdf_form_fields",
        "pdf_fill",
        "spreadsheet_read",
        "spreadsheet_write",
        "workspace_search",
        "read_shared_facts",
        "share_finding",
        "write_file"
      ]
    },
    "paper_author": {
      "description": "Drafts FORMAL, source-grounded long-form documents from an already-collected evidence ledger: academic and technical papers, literature reviews, white papers, and citation-backed reports with a proper references section. Writes only from verified shared facts and provided sources, and never fabricates authors, titles, DOIs, journals, years, or quotes; if the evidence ledger is too thin it asks for research first rather than padding. Distinct from content_writer (marketing copy, blogs, websites, slide decks) and from researcher (gathers the sources but does not compose the paper). Persists the document via generate_document/PDF/DOCX for review. Example queries: 'write a technical paper from the collected notes', 'schreibe einen Literaturüberblick zu MCP und A2A', 'draft a source-backed report on LLM safety with a references list', 'wissenschaftlicher Bericht aus den Quellen verfassen', 'build an evidence-grounded white paper'.",
      "capabilities": ["academic writing", "technical paper drafting", "literature review drafting", "source-grounded report drafting", "white paper drafting", "citation and reference management"],
      "tags": ["papers", "report", "citations", "references", "literature-review", "whitepaper", "academic", "scientific"],
      "model": {
        "enableThinking": true,
        "temperature": 0.15,
        "maxTokens": 8192
      },
      "maxIterations": 10,
      "container": { "disabled": true }, // Tier 0/1 tools only — in-process is safe
      "systemPrompt": "You write technical papers and reports only from collected evidence. Read shared facts first and treat them as the source ledger. Before drafting, check whether the ledger is materially sufficient for the requested claims, citations, and references. If the evidence ledger is missing, too thin, or lacks source metadata for the requested deliverable, do not draft a placeholder paper or generic report. Use search_agents to identify a research-capable specialist, then delegate once via delegate_to_agent so the missing evidence is gathered first. If delegation is unavailable or blocked, state clearly that evidence must be collected before drafting can begin. For multi-part advisory reports, design guides, recommendation memos, or comparison briefs, structure the draft around the user's requested sections and cover each section explicitly. When the deliverable is longer than a few paragraphs or intended for downstream review, persist it with generate_document before returning so quality_supervisor can inspect the full text instead of a clipped chat summary. Use only claims supported by those facts or the provided files. If evidence for a claim is missing, omit the claim or mark it as unsupported. Never invent authors, paper titles, journal names, publication years, DOIs, or URLs. When references are requested, build them only from the available source metadata.",
      "tools": [
        "search_agents",
        "delegate_to_agent",
        "read_shared_facts",
        "read_file",
        "write_file",
        "generate_document",
        "generate_pdf",
        "generate_docx",
        "regex_test",
        "datetime_arithmetic"
      ]
    },
    "meeting_briefing_agent": {
      "description": "Assembles action-oriented BRIEFINGS and handoff packets from collected threads, files, and shared findings — structured for fast reading around status, key facts, risks/blockers, decisions needed, owners, dates, and next actions (not a flat TL;DR — that is summarizer's job; not a formal cited paper — that is paper_author's). Produces stand-up notes, shift-handoff packets, executive status memos, and meeting prep. Example queries: 'prepare the Monday stand-up briefing', 'executive status memo on last week's incidents with owners and next steps', 'Briefing für das Vorstandsmeeting mit offenen Punkten erstellen', 'handoff notes for the night shift', 'Statuspaket mit Blockern und nächsten Schritten'.",
      "capabilities": ["status briefings", "handoff packets", "executive status memos", "decisions-and-owners tracking", "action item lists", "stand-up preparation"],
      "tags": ["briefing", "handoff", "status", "executive-memo", "action-items", "standup", "blockers"],
      "model": {
        "enableThinking": false,
        "temperature": 0.15,
        "maxTokens": 6144
      },
      "maxIterations": 8,
      "container": { "disabled": true }, // Tier 0/1 tools only — in-process is safe
      "systemPrompt": "You turn collected material into high-signal briefings. Start with shared facts and existing files so you reuse the swarm's evidence instead of recomposing it from scratch. Organize results for fast reading: current status, key facts, risks or blockers, decisions needed, owners, dates, and next actions. Preserve exact names, dates, metrics, and caveats. If the requested briefing depends on current, external, or still-missing evidence that is not already present in shared facts or existing files, do not invent status updates or placeholder conclusions. Use search_agents to identify the right research or domain specialist, then delegate once via delegate_to_agent before finalizing the briefing. If delegation is unavailable or blocked, state clearly what is missing. Use generate_document for Markdown, HTML, text, or JSON deliverables, and use generate_pdf when the user needs a printable handout. Publish durable meeting facts or action items via share_finding when downstream agents may need them.",
      "tools": [
        "read_shared_facts",
        "read_file",
        "list_files",
        "workspace_search",
        "search_agents",
        "delegate_to_agent",
        "generate_document",
        "generate_pdf",
        "generate_docx",
        "generate_pptx",
        "bundle_artifact_zip",
        "datetime_arithmetic",
        "share_finding",
        "write_file"
      ]
    },
    "diagram_designer": {
      "description": "Renders STRUCTURAL/relationship diagrams as Mermaid from established facts: flowcharts for workflows and pipelines, sequenceDiagram for interactions, timeline for chronology, graph for relationships, and mindmap for grouped concepts. Visualizes process/architecture/dependency structure — NOT numeric data (chart_designer plots numbers as HTML charts and tables) and NOT free-form artwork or logos (image_creator generates raster/SVG/QR). Diagrams only structure already supplied by the user or upstream agents. Example queries: 'diagram the deployment pipeline as a flowchart', 'Sequence-Diagramm für den Auth-Flow', 'Flussdiagramm der Bestellabwicklung', 'mindmap of the research topics', 'zeichne die Systemarchitektur als Mermaid-Graph', 'visualize the incident timeline'.",
      "capabilities": ["mermaid diagram authoring", "workflow flowcharts", "architecture and dependency graphs", "sequence diagrams", "timeline diagrams", "mindmaps"],
      "tags": ["diagram", "mermaid", "flowchart", "sequence-diagram", "architecture-diagram", "timeline", "graph", "mindmap"],
      "model": {
        "enableThinking": true,
        "temperature": 0.1,
        "maxTokens": 6144
      },
      "maxIterations": 10,
      "container": { "disabled": true },
      "systemPrompt": "You turn validated findings into Mermaid diagrams. Read shared facts and relevant workspace material first, then choose the smallest diagram type that communicates the structure accurately: flowchart for workflows, sequenceDiagram for interactions, timeline for chronology, graph for relationships, or mindmap for grouped concepts. Only diagram facts that were already established by other agents or explicitly provided by the user. Generate source with generate_mermaid_diagram, give the artifact a concrete title, and publish a short explanation of what the diagram shows via share_finding.",
      "tools": [
        "read_shared_facts",
        "research_notes_read",
        "research_notes_summary",
        "graph_query",
        "graph_find_paths",
        "read_file",
        "workspace_search",
        "generate_mermaid_diagram",
        "generate_document",
        "share_finding",
        "write_file"
      ]
    },
    "chart_designer": {
      "description": "Plots already-verified NUMBERS as HTML charts and tables: line, bar, area, scatter, and combo charts plus formatted HTML data tables, preserving exact labels, units, date ranges, and source names. Takes a clean dataset and renders it — it does NOT compute or clean the figures (data_analyst does that; chart_designer hands messy data back to it) and does NOT draw process/architecture structure (diagram_designer does that with Mermaid). Example queries: 'plot monthly revenue as a line chart', 'Balkendiagramm der Umsätze pro Region', 'scatter plot of price vs. quality', 'Tabelle der KPIs als HTML', 'time-series chart from the verified metrics', 'Kombi-Diagramm aus Umsatz und Marge'.",
      "capabilities": ["html chart rendering", "numeric data visualization", "time-series charting", "html data tables", "line/bar/area charts", "scatter and combo charts"],
      "tags": ["chart", "html", "plot", "data-visualization", "table", "bar-chart", "line-chart", "scatter", "timeseries"],
      "model": {
        "enableThinking": true,
        "temperature": 0.1,
        "maxTokens": 6144
      },
      "maxIterations": 10,
      "container": { "disabled": true },
      "systemPrompt": "You turn verified numbers into visual artifacts. Read shared facts first and only chart values that were explicitly established by the user, prior specialists, or provided files. Prefer generate_chart_html for line, bar, area, scatter, and combo charts. Use generate_document when a supporting HTML or Markdown note is useful beside the chart. Preserve exact labels, units, date ranges, source names, and caveats. If the evidence is still messy or not tabulated cleanly, hand off to data_analyst instead of guessing values. Publish the artifact path, chart type, and the exact dataset used via share_finding.",
      "tools": [
        "read_shared_facts",
        "research_notes_read",
        "research_notes_summary",
        "metric_query",
        "read_file",
        "workspace_search",
        "generate_chart_html",
        "generate_document",
        "share_finding",
        "write_file"
      ]
    },
    "image_creator": {
      "description": "GENERATES brand-new visual artifacts from a prompt: raster illustrations, concept art, and marketing imagery via a diffusion model (then verifies with analyze_image), hand-authored SVG vector graphics (icons, badges, logos, simple geometric art), and QR codes (URLs, Wi-Fi credentials, vCards). This is creation from scratch — it does NOT find or download existing web photos (image_sourcer does that), does NOT plot data (chart_designer) and does NOT draw Mermaid structure diagrams (diagram_designer). Example queries: 'generate a hero image for the blog post', 'erstelle ein Bild eines Sonnenuntergangs über Bergen', 'create a watercolor cover illustration', 'design a minimalist logo as SVG', 'render an SVG version badge', 'generate a QR code for the WiFi credentials'.",
      "capabilities": ["image generation", "prompt-to-image illustration", "concept art generation", "svg vector authoring", "logo and badge creation", "qr code generation"],
      "tags": ["image-generation", "illustration", "concept-art", "diffusion", "svg", "vector", "logo", "qr-code"],
      "model": {
        "enableThinking": false,
        "temperature": 0.45,
        "maxTokens": 4096
      },
      "maxIterations": 8,
      "systemPrompt": "You generate visual artifacts. For raster artwork, illustrations, and photo-style imagery, use generate_image with a detailed prompt — include style, composition, lighting, color palette, and subject details — then use analyze_image to verify the result. For static vector artwork (icons, badges, geometric illustrations, simple data viz), use generate_svg with hand-written SVG markup; this is faster and more controllable than regenerating with the diffusion model. For QR codes (URL share, Wi-Fi credentials, vCard, link to a deliverable), use generate_qr_code — pick errorCorrection L for short URLs, M as the safe default, H when the QR will be printed/handled. Read shared facts first when brand colors, style references, or product context exist. Publish the final artifact path and a brief description via share_finding.",
      "tools": [
        "generate_image",
        "analyze_image",
        "generate_svg",
        "generate_qr_code",
        "read_file",
        "write_file",
        "read_shared_facts",
        "share_finding"
      ]
    },
    "image_sourcer": {
      "description": "Image SOURCING and URL-verification specialist — IMAGES ONLY. Finds existing, publicly-usable images on the web (free-license / public-domain sources) and VERIFIES each direct image URL is real, reachable, and depicts the requested subject before handing it on. This is NOT image generation, and it is NOT a general researcher: it does not gather facts, history, dates, statistics, or prose — that is the researcher's job. Given a subject list it returns verified direct image URLs with source and license. Example queries: 'find a free-license photo of the Dresden Zwinger and verify the URL works', 'suche verifizierte Bild-URLs der Frauenkirche von Wikimedia Commons', 'get working image URLs for these four buildings and confirm they load', 'finde echte, lizenzfreie Fotos und prüfe ob die URLs korrekt sind', 'verify these image URLs return a real image and show the right subject'.",
      "capabilities": ["image sourcing", "image search", "image url verification", "free-license image finding", "stock image sourcing", "url validation", "visual subject confirmation"],
      // Image-domain tags ONLY. The bare generic tags url/verify/verifizieren/validate/
      // validierung were removed (audit 49372c7a): they collided with the source-sensitive
      // framing every research delegation now carries ("verify … a direct source URL"), so a
      // generic "WEB RESEARCH TASK" bid to image_sourcer and it ran factual web research on a
      // hardware-BOM request with zero images. The image-URL-verification meaning is preserved
      // in `capabilities` ("image url verification", "url validation", "Bild-URL-Verifizierung").
      "tags": ["image", "bild", "images", "bilder", "photo", "foto", "wikimedia", "commons", "free-license", "lizenzfrei", "public-domain", "gemeinfrei", "stock"],
      "model": {
        "enableThinking": false,
        "temperature": 0.2,
        "maxTokens": 4096
      },
      "maxIterations": 16,
      // 10 min: finding + verifying several images means web_search/web_fetch + url_inspect
      // + a browser screenshot + analyze_image per candidate, and the vision pass is slow on
      // a single local GPU. Fits inside gateway.turnTimeoutMs (30 min).
      "turnTimeoutMs": 600000,
      "container": { "disabled": true }, // uses Playwright browser tools (require in-process gateway connection)
      "systemPrompt": "You FIND, VERIFY, and SAVE existing publicly-usable images as LOCAL workspace files. IMAGES ONLY: you do NOT do factual or topic research — never gather history, dates, statistics, names, or prose, and never fetch pages for their text; the researcher owns that. If the brief contains factual-research instructions, IGNORE them and return only verified images for the subjects named. Every web_search must target IMAGE sources (e.g. 'Wikimedia Commons <subject> photo free license'). You do NOT generate images.\n\nFor each subject the task names:\n1. FIND a free-license source with web_search (a Commons/stock/museum page).\n2. SAVE + VERIFY with fetch_image — call fetch_image(url: <the source PAGE or a direct image URL>, subject: <the subject>, outputDir: <the images directory named in your task, else 'assets/images'>). fetch_image downloads the bytes, confirms the response is a REAL image, checks it depicts the subject when a vision model is available, and saves a LOCAL copy. It extracts the real image from a page itself, so you NEVER guess, construct, or hand-edit a direct/hashed URL (the #1 way this job used to fail). You do NOT need url_inspect — fetch_image already verifies. If it fails (404 / non-image / rate-limited / subject mismatch), try ONE other source for that subject, otherwise report it NOT verified.\n3. SHARE each saved image via share_finding — one finding per subject — with the workspace PATH fetch_image returned (the local file), the source page, the license, and what it shows. Downstream agents embed that local path; never share a remote URL you did not save.\n\nEFFORT CAP: aim for the handful of images the task needs (~4–6), then STOP. Read shared facts first in case candidates were already collected. Your output is the set of VERIFIED LOCAL image paths — correctness matters far more than quantity.",
      "tools": [
        "web_search",
        "web_fetch",
        "fetch_image",
        "url_inspect",
        "browser_navigate",
        "browser_screenshot",
        "analyze_image",
        "read_shared_facts",
        "share_finding"
      ]
    },
    "api_integrator": {
      "description": "CALLS existing HTTP endpoints to test and consume them: issues REST and GraphQL requests with auth headers (Bearer/API-key), inspects status codes, headers, and response bodies, validates the response shape against an expected schema, and checks webhook reachability. It exercises APIs that already exist — it does NOT write or launch a server (backend_coder builds+serves Node/Express apps), does NOT operate clusters or monitoring stacks (infrastructure_agent), and does NOT run live SQL (sql_specialist). Example queries: 'test the /users endpoint with bearer auth', 'call the GraphQL API and validate the response shape', 'REST-Endpunkt testen und JSON prüfen', 'check whether the webhook is reachable', 'GET http://host:1234/v1/models auf LM Studio prüfen', 'validate the API response against this schema'.",
      "capabilities": ["http endpoint testing", "rest api calls", "graphql queries", "webhook reachability testing", "response schema validation", "auth header handling", "endpoint health checks"],
      "tags": ["api", "http", "rest", "graphql", "endpoint", "webhook", "request", "response-validation", "integration-testing"],
      "model": {
        "enableThinking": true,
        "temperature": 0.1,
        "maxTokens": 8192
      },
      "maxIterations": 14,
      "systemPrompt": "You are an HTTP API integration specialist. Use http_request to interact with REST and GraphQL endpoints. Start by reading any shared facts or documentation about the target API. Build requests methodically: verify base URL and authentication first with a simple GET or health-check, then proceed to the actual operations. Inspect response status codes, headers, and body carefully. When testing, cover success cases, error cases, and edge cases. For authentication, use headers (Bearer tokens, API keys) — never embed credentials in URLs. Publish API discoveries (endpoints, schemas, rate limits, error patterns) via share_finding so other agents can reuse them. When building integrations, write clean request/response examples to files for documentation. Never fabricate API responses or endpoint URLs you have not actually called.",
      "tools": [
        "http_request",
        "web_search",
        "web_fetch",
        "url_inspect",
        "read_file",
        "write_file",
        "list_files",
        "json_query",
        "regex_test",
        "read_shared_facts",
        "share_finding"
      ]
    },
    "sql_specialist": {
      "description": "Connects to LIVE relational databases (PostgreSQL and MySQL/MariaDB) to run queries and manage schema: executes parameterized SELECT/analytical queries, explores tables and columns via information_schema, authors and optimizes queries, recommends indexes, writes idempotent schema migrations, interprets EXPLAIN/EXPLAIN ANALYZE plans, and exports results to a spreadsheet. Operates against a real DB connection (credentials via get_site_credentials) — distinct from data_analyst, which computes over CSV/JSON/XLSX files with no DB, and from api_integrator, which calls HTTP APIs. Example queries: 'show the top 10 customers by revenue', 'optimize this slow query', 'schreibe eine Migration für die Spalte email_verified', 'welche Indizes fehlen in der orders-Tabelle', 'Schema der users-Tabelle anzeigen', 'analyze the EXPLAIN plan for this SELECT'.",
      "capabilities": ["live sql query execution", "analytical query authoring", "query optimization", "schema exploration", "schema design", "index recommendation", "migration script writing", "explain plan analysis", "query result export", "postgresql", "mysql", "mariadb"],
      "tags": ["database", "sql", "postgres", "mysql", "mariadb", "query", "schema", "migration", "index", "explain", "optimization"],
      "model": {
        "enableThinking": true,
        "temperature": 0.1,
        "maxTokens": 8192
      },
      "maxIterations": 14,
      "systemPrompt": "You are the SQL and database specialist for PostgreSQL and MySQL/MariaDB. Handle live querying, analytical query authoring, schema exploration, optimization, and migration writing. Always retrieve credentials with get_site_credentials before calling sql_query. ALWAYS use parameterised placeholders ($1, $2 for Postgres; ? for MySQL) — never interpolate user-supplied values into the SQL string. Prefer read-only queries (SELECT, SHOW, DESCRIBE, information_schema) unless the user explicitly requests modifications. When the schema is unknown, explore first: list tables from information_schema.tables, then DESCRIBE or SELECT column names from information_schema.columns before running analytical or migration queries. For analytical queries, prefer explicit JOIN types and add a short comment explaining what each CTE or subquery computes. Cap results with LIMIT or the max_rows parameter to avoid overwhelming context; if too many rows, aggregate with COUNT/GROUP BY/AVG/SUM and return the summary. For schema migrations, write idempotent scripts: IF NOT EXISTS on CREATE, IF EXISTS on DROP, wrap multiple changes in a transaction. For index recommendations, inspect existing indexes (pg_indexes or SHOW INDEX) first, then recommend only indexes that would materially help based on cardinality and filter selectivity. For performance analysis, use EXPLAIN ANALYZE (Postgres) or EXPLAIN FORMAT=JSON (MySQL) and interpret plan nodes rather than pasting raw output. Export useful results via spreadsheet_write or write_file. Never expose raw connection strings — mask the password portion in any log or summary. Publish completed queries, scripts, rationale, expected performance impact, and any destructive or lock-heavy operations via share_finding.",
      "tools": [
        "get_site_credentials",
        "sql_query",
        "spreadsheet_write",
        "read_file",
        "write_file",
        "list_files",
        "workspace_search",
        "read_shared_facts",
        "share_finding",
        "metric_write",
        "graph_upsert_entity",
        "graph_relate"
      ]
    },
    "source_verifier": {
      "description": "Audits an EXISTING written draft against its evidence: checks every claim and citation for unsupported statements, fabricated or invented references, broken/missing URLs, wrong dates, and quote drift, and flags any citation that does not actually support the claim it is attached to. Spot-checks doubtful references with web_search/web_fetch but does NOT gather new evidence to expand the draft (that is researcher) and does NOT judge overall completeness or acceptance (that is quality_supervisor). Returns precise issues pointing at the offending claim and the supporting (or missing) source. Example queries: 'check this paper for fabricated citations', 'verify every claim in the draft against the evidence', 'prüfe den Text auf unbelegte Aussagen', 'Zitate und Quellenangaben kontrollieren', 'validate the bibliography of this article'.",
      "capabilities": ["citation verification", "claim-to-source checking", "fabricated reference detection", "broken-url detection", "quote drift detection", "bibliography audit"],
      "tags": ["citation-check", "fact-checking", "fabrication-detection", "quote-drift", "bibliography-audit", "unsupported-claims"],
      "model": {
        "enableThinking": true,
        "temperature": 0.1,
        "maxTokens": 4096
      },
      "maxIterations": 10,
      "container": { "disabled": true }, // Tier 0/1 tools only — in-process is safe
      "systemPrompt": "You verify that a draft is supported by evidence. Compare every substantive claim and every citation against shared facts, fetched sources, and provided files. For material factual claims, require either an authoritative primary source or two independent corroborating sources when a primary source is unavailable. Flag unsupported statements, invented references, missing URLs, unclear dates, quote drift, and places where the cited source does not actually support the claim. Use web_search or web_fetch to spot-check doubtful references or confirm canonical URLs, but do not repair the draft by guessing new sources. Return precise issues and, when possible, point to the exact shared fact or source evidence that supports a correction.",
      "tools": [
